<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roger Borsa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1060–<a href="/years/1111">1111</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roger Borsa was duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> from 1085, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a> in preference to his elder half-brother Bohemond, who was given Taranto. His rule was weakened by rebellious barons and he relied on his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-i-of-sicily">Roger I of Sicily</a>. He was succeeded by his son William II.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roger-borsa.jpg" alt="Roger Borsa" width="300" height="148" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salerno_Follaro_75001317.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Roger was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichelgaita">Sikelgaita</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> noblewoman. His mother arranged for him to succeed his father in place of Guiscard's eldest son by another wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemond-i-of-antioch">Bohemond of Taranto</a>. His nickname Borsa means purse and came from his early habit of counting and recounting his money. In 1073 Sikelgaita had Roger proclaimed heir after Guiscard fell ill at Trani. Only his cousin Abelard dissented, claiming to be the rightful heir to the duchy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Roger accompanied his father on the campaign to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 1084 and was still there when Guiscard died on 17 July 1085 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a>. Bohemond was meant to take the Greek possessions and Roger the Italian ones, but Bohemond was in Salerno and Roger in Greece. Roger returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with his mother, and with the support of his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-i-of-sicily">Roger I of Sicily</a> was recognised as duke in September. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> descent made him a more attractive candidate than his Norman half-brother, who fled to Capua.</p><p>With the support of Jordan I of Capua, Bohemond rebelled and took Oria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otranto">Otranto</a> and Taranto. Peace came in March 1086 and the brothers acted as co-rulers, but Bohemond renewed the war in late summer 1087 with some of Roger's vassals and defeated him at Fragneto. Pope Urban II mediated and Bohemond received Taranto and other possessions, together with the homage of Geoffrey of Conversano. In September 1089 Urban invested Roger with the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> at the Synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melfi">Melfi</a>. He allowed some barons to mint coins and planned to build up towns in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> by granting charters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In May 1098 Roger and his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-i-of-sicily">Roger I of Sicily</a> besieged Capua at the request of Prince Richard II of Capua, who then did homage to the duke, though his successors ignored it. In October 1104 Roger besieged William, Count of Monte Sant'Angelo, who had pledged himself to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, and abolished the county. In 1092 he had married Adela, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-i-count-of-flanders">Robert I, Count of Flanders</a> and widow of Canute IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. He died on 22 February 1111 and was buried in Salerno cathedral. His children included a son William, and Adela's son Louis died young in 1094.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roger Borsa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Byzantine emperor sent ambassadors, and the Norman duke <b>Roger Borsa</b> attended in person.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-10">10 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After Guiscard's death in 1085, his son <b>Roger Borsa</b> and grandson William II struggled to control rebellious barons.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-apulia-and-calabria">Duchy of Apulia and Calabria</a></li><li><blockquote>After Robert Guiscard's death the Norman inheritance was divided, and in 1088 his eldest son Bohemond received Taranto and much of the heel of Italy as compensation for losing the Duchy of Apulia and Calabria to his half-brother <b>Roger Borsa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-taranto">Principality of Taranto</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Salerno, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.75,14.95&amp;year=1093" aria-label="Campania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4916 708 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Roger Borsa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5366 978h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5366 978h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.75,14.95&amp;year=1093">What was here in 1093? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
